{
	"comment": "Shared conformance vectors for the access model's permission decision: engine/server/accesschain.go (accessChains.holds and accessChains.holdsThroughEach) and client/src/access.ts (canAt) must reach the same verdict on every case both can decide. A caller holds a permission at a row when it holds it at the workspace root, at the row itself, or at any row above the row: a row its access parents name, a row their access parents name, and so on. `access` maps each permission the caller holds to where: `root` for the workspace root, `anchors` as entity name to row keys; null means no summary has reached the client, which only the client can be missing. A case gives the rows above the row in one of two forms. `chain` lists them for a row with one parent at each step, nearest first: the row its access_parent names, the row that row's access_parent names, and so on, until a row whose entity has no access parent or whose reference is unset; a chain that ends in the string \"unknown\" stops at a row the client does not hold. `graph` lists the rows that have parents, each with `parents`, the rows its access parents name, or the string \"unknown\" for a row the client does not hold; a row the graph does not list has no parents. The server never lacks a row. With `each` set, the case asks whether the caller holds the permission through each parent of the row on its own: at the root, at the row itself, or at that parent or a row above it. A create needs a permission through each parent the row names. `entitiesAbove`, where a case gives it, maps an entity to the entities whose rows can sit above a row of it, on any path and at any depth: a path that stops at a row the client does not hold answers \"no\" when the caller holds the permission at no row of an entity above that row. Without it, or for an entity it does not list, such a path is \"unknown\". `want` is \"yes\", \"no\" or \"unknown\"; the engine checks the cases whose `access` is not null and that name no \"unknown\" row, where \"yes\" means it holds the permission.",
